<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 4: Cleaning and Recoding Data                            -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Opens with the West Africa Ebola outbreak (2014-2016), drawing on -->
<!--  a real, verified paper:                                          -->
<!--    - Roshania et al. (2016), Global Health: Science and Practice   -->
<!--      4(3), 394-409 (IMC's multicountry clinical data collection)   -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-cleaning-recoding" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Cleaning and Recoding Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-cleaning-recoding">
    <p>
      In the last chapter, we explored the Kenya Road Accidents database, and
      noticed lots of problems along the way: county names spelled in two
      different ways, ages given as groups as well as numbers, and so on.
      We made a note of them, and moved on.
      Now it's time to actually do something about them!
    </p>

    <p>
      Data cleaning is all the work that goes into getting a dataset ready for
      analysis.
      That means fixing inconsistencies, dealing with missing values, recoding
      variables into more useful forms, and checking that the data really
      contains what it says it contains.
      It's so easy to underestimate how much this matters.
      While you're cleaning, you're making decisions all the time: what counts
      as a duplicate, what a blank cell means, how to group a free-text
      description.
      And every one of those decisions shapes every result that comes after.
      If you skip them, rush them, or leave them to software without checking,
      you can end up with an answer that's confident, precise, and completely
      wrong.
    </p>

    <p>
      Something like this happened, on a huge scale, during the deadliest Ebola
      outbreak ever recorded.
      So that's where we're going to start.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: Ebola, West Africa 2014-2016                          -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-ebola-data">
    <title>A Preventable Disaster</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Between 2014 and 2016, the largest Ebola epidemic ever recorded swept
        through Guinea, Liberia, and Sierra Leone, and killed more than 11,000
        people.
        When you're fighting an epidemic on that scale, you need to know, as
        close to real time as you possibly can, where new cases are appearing,
        how fast they're growing, and who each infected person has been in
        contact with.
      </p>

      <p>
        The International Medical Corps ran five Ebola Treatment Units across
        Liberia and Sierra Leone.
        Over the course of the epidemic, they treated more than 2,500 patients,
        and built up over 25,000 pages of clinical, epidemiological, and
        operational data.
        Part of the reason for collecting all of this was to help the world
        respond better to future epidemics.
        But what they actually found was that even one well-resourced
        organisation, running its own units under its own protocols, couldn't
        keep its data consistent (Roshania et al., 2016).
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-collection">
      <title>Getting the data out</title>

      <p>
        Just think about the conditions this data was collected in.
        Information about each patient's daily symptoms and treatment was
        recorded at their bedside, inside the high-risk zone, where clinicians
        were wearing full personal protective equipment (PPE) the whole time.
        Between patients, the standard practice was to wash gloved hands in
        chlorine solution, without drying them.
        That chlorine gradually soaked into the paper, and made the forms harder
        and harder to read.
        And because any paper in the high-risk zone could be contaminated, none
        of it could safely come back out.
      </p>

      <p>
        So how do you get the data out?
        Staff had three options.
        They could read the values out across the fence to a colleague, who
        copied them down on the other side.
        They could photograph the forms from across the barrier.
        Or they could send someone back into the zone, in full PPE, just to
        photograph paperwork with a waterproof camera that could be
        decontaminated afterwards.
        Every one of these options was error-prone in its own way.
        Numbers could be misheard across the fence, photos could be blurry, and
        values could be skipped when copying them out.
        So you had forms that were hard to read to begin with, followed by a
        copying step that added even more chances for errors.
        And any error that got through without being spotted became part of the
        dataset, and so part of the analysis.
      </p>

      <p>
        The organisation did take steps to limit these errors, like setting up
        data validation in Excel, and running audits.
        Their first quality audit compared a random sample of patient records,
        field by field, with the original scanned charts.
        The error rates were so high that large sections of the dataset had to
        be re-entered by hand.
        There weren't the resources to redo everything, so they re-entered the
        triage data for all patients, but only re-entered the more detailed
        daily treatment data for patients who were confirmed to have Ebola.
        After all that effort, they got to around 99% accuracy.
      </p>

      <p>
        The problem is that this cleaning happened months later, long after the
        patients had been treated and the outbreak had moved on.
        During the epidemic itself, when people were making real-time decisions
        about where to send resources and how to respond, they were working from
        the uncleaned data.
        Cleaning after the fact is definitely better than not cleaning at all.
        But it's no substitute for getting the data right in the first place.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-inconsistency">
      <title>Inconsistency across treatment units</title>

      <p>
        Some variables, like whether the patient had recently eaten bush meat,
        or where they'd travelled, were recorded at one unit but not at another.
        So when it came to combining the datasets, someone had to decide what to
        do about those gaps.
        This is a <term>missing data</term> problem: information that should be
        there isn't, and any analysis has to either work around it, or make
        assumptions about it.
        And those assumptions really matter!
        A patient who was never asked about bush meat is very different from a
        patient who was asked and said no.
        We look at missing data in much more detail in <xref
        ref="ch-handling-missing-values"/>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Other variables were recorded inconsistently.
        For example, <q>red injected eyes</q>, <q>conjunctivitis</q>, and
        <q>hemorrhagic eyes</q> were all used for the same symptom.
        Before anyone could analyse the data, these had to be
        <term>recoded</term> by hand into one consistent variable.
        Without that recoding, if you'd counted the patients with eye symptoms,
        you'd have got the wrong answer, because the same information had been
        recorded in three different ways.
        This is an <term>inconsistent values</term> problem: the data is there,
        but you can't use it until it's been standardised.
      </p>

      <p>
        The quality of data entry also changed over time, even within the same
        unit, depending on how bad the outbreak was and how many staff were
        around.
        More patients meant more data to enter, but the number of staff didn't
        grow to match.
        So data quality was most likely to slip at the peak of the outbreak,
        which is exactly when accurate data mattered most.
      </p>

      <p>
        These are all problems that <term>data cleaning</term> deals with.
        Data cleaning is the process of finding and fixing problems in a dataset
        before you analyse it: standardising inconsistent values, handling
        missing data, correcting errors that crept in during collection or
        copying, and making sure that data from different sources can be
        combined reliably.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-consequences">
      <title>What the inconsistency cost</title>

      <p>
        Each treatment unit collected its data independently, but some questions
        could only be answered by looking across all five units together.
        Out of everyone admitted with Ebola, how many died?
        If someone was identified as a contact of a patient at one unit, did
        they later turn up as a patient at another?
        These are exactly the questions you need answered quickly in an
        epidemic, and inconsistent data made them slow and unreliable to answer.
      </p>

      <p>
        Looking back, the organisation's own assessment was really honest:
        <q>much time and effort could have been saved through closer oversight
        and real-time quality assurance assessments in the field.</q>
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-ebola-cleaning">
        <title>Cleaning was essential work here</title>

        <p>
          Inconsistent variable names, missing values, incompatible formats, and
          errors from copying data out: these are exactly the kinds of problems
          data cleaning is for.
          When the stakes are low, it's easy to brush them off as technical
          nuisances.
          But they can change what the data tells you in so many ways.
          In the Ebola response, messy data meant nobody could say reliably what
          fraction of patients were surviving.
          It meant not being able to track whether contacts in one area were
          becoming cases in another.
          And it meant not being able to see the epidemic clearly enough to
          respond to it well.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Roshania, R.
        et al.
        (2016).
        <q>Successful Implementation of a Multicountry Clinical 
    Surveillance and Data Collection System for Ebola Virus Disease 
    in West Africa: Findings and Lessons Learned.</q>
        <em>Global Health: Science and Practice</em>, 4(3), 394 <ndash/> 409.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 2: the STACK question that exemplifies this              -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-stack-crop-example">
    <title>STACK Question Here</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Obviously, the scale is nowhere near the same, but the mistake at the
        heart of the Ebola data comes up all the time in much smaller datasets:
        deciding what odd-looking values mean, without really noticing that
        you're making a decision at all.
        Before you can ask a dataset a question, you need to know whether the
        data is ready to be asked.
        The next exercise gives you the chance to do exactly that.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch4-stack">
      <title>A survey of 100 farmers</title>

      <p>
        Here's a survey of 100 farmers.
        The dataset has three variables: <c>Age</c> (the age of the farmer),
        <c>Variety</c> (the crop variety they planted), and <c>Yield</c> (their
        harvest yield).
        We want to find out whether younger and older farmers tend to plant
        different varieties, and whether that affects their yields.
      </p>

      <p>
        But before we can ask that, we need to make sure the data is ready for
        analysis.
        Download the dataset from the STACK question, and work through the
        questions.
        You'll find that some of them have more than one sensible answer, and
        that the answer depends on decisions you make about the data first.
        That's completely normal!
      </p>

      <p>
        TODO: insert STACK question
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: it happened again, elsewhere                          -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-other-disasters">
    <title>When the data looks fine but isn't</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The Ebola story involved lots of failures happening at once: duplicate
        records, inconsistent formats, data lost in transmission, missing
        values, and using the wrong tools for the job.
        But nothing in the output announced that anything was wrong.
        The next two stories have exactly the same shape.
        In both, there's a process that seems to be working fine, producing
        results that look completely legitimate, while underneath, something has
        quietly broken.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-phe-pipeline">
      <title>
        COVID-19 in England: the ceiling nobody was watching out for
      </title>

      <p>
        During the COVID-19 pandemic, lots of countries ran contact tracing
        programmes.
        When someone tested positive, public health workers would get in touch
        with everyone that person had been in close contact with, and ask them
        to isolate.
        The faster a positive case was reported and their contacts traced, the
        fewer people they were likely to infect in the meantime.
      </p>

      <p>
        In England, positive test results from the testing labs were sent out as
        CSV files.
        Those files were imported into a template built on the old, pre-2007
        Excel <c>.xls</c> format, and then passed on to contact tracers.
        This had been working well for several months.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then, in late September 2020, the daily case counts being reported were
        lower than other indicators suggested they should be.
        But the files were still coming in, and contact tracers were still
        working through them.
        From the outside, the system looked like it was doing its job, just with
        slightly better numbers than expected, at a time when the outbreak was
        actually growing.
      </p>

      <p>
        So what was going on?
        Public Health England (PHE), the government body responsible for public
        health in England, had set up an automated process to pull CSV files
        from commercial testing labs into Excel templates, which were then sent
        on to the NHS Test and Trace team.
        The problem was the file format.
        The templates had been built using the pre-2007 <c>.xls</c> format,
        which has a hard limit of 65,536 rows per sheet.
        Each test result took up several rows once the template columns were
        filled in, so each template could only hold around 1,400 cases.
        When a batch of results went over that limit, the extra cases were just
        left off the file.
        There was no error message, and the template looked complete, but those
        extra cases never reached the contact tracers.
        This went on for eight days.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's why the numbers had looked manageable.
        They were lower because the outbreak had grown past the 1,400 limit, and
        the system couldn't cope.
        Every time a daily batch went over 1,400 cases, the extra cases were
        silently cut off before they reached anyone who could act on them.
        Between 25 September and 2 October 2020, 15,841 positive test results
        were lost this way.
        The people behind those results weren't told in time, and the people
        they'd been in contact with weren't traced and asked to isolate, while
        the outbreak in England was actively growing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Once the problem was found, the fix was straightforward: split the
        incoming files into smaller batches, so that no single template went
        over the row limit.
        The health secretary at the time pointed out that the system had already
        been scheduled for replacement since July.
        It just hadn't been replaced yet.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-phe-lesson">
        <title>The failure was silent</title>

        <p>
          If the system had crashed, someone would have noticed straight away.
          But a file that just stops growing, with no warning and no error
          message, looks exactly like a file that worked.
          Checking that the number of rows you exported matches the number of
          rows you expected is one of the simplest habits in data cleaning, and
          this is exactly the kind of problem it catches.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> UK Government,
        <q>Slides to accompany coronavirus 
  press conference, CMO, 31 October 2020.</q>
        Leo Kelion,
        <q>Excel: Why 
  using Microsoft's tool caused Covid-19 results to be lost,</q>
        BBC, 5 October 2020,
        <url href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/technology-54423988"/>;
        <url href="https://www.theregister.com/2020/10/05/excel_england_coronavirus_contact_error/" visual="The Register, 5 October 2020"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-gene-names">
      <title>Gene names: recoding you didn't ask for</title>

      <p>
        The last example was about data silently disappearing.
        This one is about data quietly turning into something else.
        And it happened, largely unnoticed, across an entire scientific field,
        for more than a decade!
      </p>

      <p>
        Humans have around 20,000 genes.
        To keep scientific communication clear, and databases consistent, each
        gene is given a short symbol made of letters and numbers.
        For example:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <q>Metastasis Associated Lung Adenocarcinoma Transcript 1</q> is
              given the symbol <c>MALAT1</c>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <q>Septin 2</q> is given the symbol <c>SEPT2</c>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <q>Membrane Associated Ring-CH-Type Finger 1</q> is given the
              symbol <c>MARCH1</c>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        Genomics researchers regularly list hundreds of these symbols in
        spreadsheets as part of their published research.
        And Microsoft Excel, with its default settings, tries to be helpful.
        If a cell looks like it contains a date, Excel quietly converts it into
        a date format, instead of keeping what was actually typed.
        So <c>SEPT2</c> looks enough like <q>2 September</q> that Excel turns it
        into <c>2-Sep</c>.
        <c>MARCH1</c> becomes <c>1-Mar</c>.
        In total, 27 different genes have been found that Excel mistakes for
        dates.
        And the real problem?
        Excel doesn't give you any warning.
        It just replaces the gene with a date, thinking it's helping, when it
        has nothing to do with the biology at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        In 2016, researchers Mark Ziemann, Yotam Eren, and Assam El-Osta looked
        at 3,597 papers published between 2005 and 2015 in 18 leading genomics
        journals.
        These were all peer-reviewed papers in top journals: exactly the kind of
        work other researchers trust and build on.
        Of the papers that included a supplementary Excel gene list, roughly
        <term>one in five</term> had at least one gene name silently corrupted
        into a date or a number.
        One in five!
      </p>

      <p>
        So why does this matter?
        A gene symbol is the key that links a finding in one paper to the same
        gene in every other paper, database, and analysis tool.
        Imagine you download a supplementary file so you can build on someone
        else's work.
        You search for <c>SEPT2</c> and find nothing, because the file says
        <c>2-Sep</c> instead.
        Or worse, you don't notice the problem at all, carry the wrong value
        into your own analysis, and publish results built on a gene list that
        was silently wrong from the very start.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two things really stand out to us here.
        First, nobody decided to recode <c>SEPT2</c> into a date.
        The software did it for them, and it went unnoticed for years at a time,
        across a whole discipline.
        Second, this wasn't a new problem.
        It had been documented, with workarounds, as early as 2004, over a
        decade before Ziemann et al. looked into it.
        People knew about the problem, and a fix had been suggested.
        And yet the corruption carried on anyway.
      </p>

      <p>
        By 2020, the problem was so serious that the HUGO Gene Nomenclature
        Committee (HGNC), the international body that approves and standardises
        human gene names, renamed twenty-seven genes, just to stop this from
        happening.
        For example, <c>MARCH1</c> became <c>MARCHF1</c>, and <c>SEPT1</c>
        became <c>SEPTIN1</c>.
        The genes themselves hadn't changed at all.
        It was simply easier to rename twenty-seven genes than to get Excel to
        stop guessing!
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-genes-lesson">
        <title>Sometimes recoding happens without you choosing it</title>

        <p>
          Every time you import a spreadsheet, a piece of software has already
          made decisions about how to read every cell (what looks like a number,
          what looks like a date, what looks like text) before you've looked at
          any of it yourself.
          So cleaning data includes more than fixing values that are obviously
          wrong.
          It also means checking that nothing was silently <q>fixed</q> for you,
          incorrectly.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Ziemann, M., Eren, Y.
        and El-Osta, A.
        (2016).
        <q>Gene name errors are widespread in the scientific 
      literature.</q>
        <em>Genome Biology</em>, 17, 177;
        <url href="https://www.genenames.org" visual="genenames.org"/> (HGNC,
        2020 renaming guidelines).
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 5: applied to the Kenya Road Accidents data             -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-kenya-accidents-r-cleaning">
    <title>Cleaning the Kenya Road Accidents Data in R</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>, we loaded the Kenya Road Accidents
        database, and started exploring it with pivot tables, summaries, and
        simple plots.
        Along the way, we spotted lots of problems: inconsistent county names,
        ambiguous times, mixed-up gender entries, and free-text accident
        descriptions that needed grouping before we could analyse them.
        Now we're going to work through those problems one by one.
        And as we go, you'll see how cleaning and exploring feed into each
        other: every round of cleaning shows us something new to look at, and
        every new look shows us something else that needs fixing.
      </p>

      <p>
        We're using the same dataset as before, with a few extra packages.
        If you haven't loaded them already:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      library(rpivotTable)
      library(readxl)
      library(dplyr)
      library(stringr)
      library(lubridate)
      library(ggplot2)
      library(purrr)
      library(tidyr)

      kenya_accidents_database &lt;- read_excel("kenya-accidents-database.xlsx")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Before we start, I have a small confession.
        It wasn't until I opened this dataset in Excel that I realised the 13th
        column was just a note someone had typed into the spreadsheet, and not
        data at all!
        I'd had a suspicion from <c>View()</c> in R, but I didn't fully get it
        until I could see the whole sheet laid out in front of me.
        I think that's worth saying.
        R is really powerful, but it doesn't show you your data the way a
        spreadsheet does.
        Using different tools together (for example, R for your analysis, and
        Excel or another spreadsheet viewer for a visual sanity check) is a
        really good habit to get into.
        Different software is good at different things.
      </p>

      <p>
        So, the first thing to do is get rid of that 13th column:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      names(kenya_accidents_database)
      kenya_accidents_database$`...13` &lt;- NULL
        </code>
      </program>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-time">
      <title>Making sense of <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c></title>

      <p>
        Let's start with the <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> column.
        It's worth taking a careful look at this one before we do anything with
        it.
        There are lots of ways to inspect it, and here are three:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # Option 1: pivot table
      rpivotTable(kenya_accidents_database, rows = "TIME 24 HOURS")

      # Option 2: unique values
      unique(kenya_accidents_database$`TIME 24 HOURS`)

      # Option 3: counts of each value
      kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
        group_by(`TIME 24 HOURS`) %&gt;%
        count() %&gt;%
        View()
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Whichever one you use, a few things stand out straight away.
      </p>

      <p>
        First, there are missing values.
        In the pivot table, these show up as a column labelled <c>NULL</c>,
        which is how <c>rpivotTable</c> displays <c>NA</c>s.
        With <c>unique()</c> or the count table, they show up as <c>NA</c>.
        Either way, we can see that for some crashes, we don't have the time.
      </p>

      <p>
        Second, there's an entry that says <c>UNKNOWN TIME</c>.
        Technically, this isn't a missing value, because someone has typed text
        into the cell.
        But it's clearly trying to tell us the same thing: nobody knew the time.
        So we can confidently treat it as missing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Third, and this is the really interesting one, there are times that look
        wrong.
        Most entries have four digits (<c>1030</c>, <c>1130</c>, <c>1915</c>),
        which is what you'd expect for 24-hour time.
        But some have only three digits (<c>140</c>, <c>630</c>), two digits
        (<c>10</c>, <c>45</c>), or even just one (<c>1</c>).
        What's going on?
        Are these typos?
        Does <c>140</c> mean <c>1400</c>, or <c>0140</c>?
      </p>

      <p>
        Our best guess is Excel (again!).
        If you type a number starting with <c>0</c> into an Excel cell formatted
        as a number, Excel quietly removes the leading zero.
        So <c>0140</c> becomes <c>140</c>, <c>0010</c> becomes <c>10</c>, and
        <c>0001</c> becomes <c>1</c>.
        If that's what happened, then any entry with fewer than four digits has
        just lost its leading zeros, and we can put them back by padding each
        value to four digits.
        The <c>NA</c> entries might then be <c>0000</c> (midnight), stripped
        away to nothing in the same way.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is an educated guess, and we can't be certain.
        In a real project, you'd get in touch with whoever looks after the data
        to check.
        For this example, let's pretend we did exactly that, and they confirmed
        that yes, the leading zeros were stripped by Excel, just like in the
        gene names story earlier in this chapter.
        With that confirmed, we can fix the column:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
        mutate(
          # Treat "UNKNOWN TIME" as NA
          `TIME 24 HOURS` = na_if(`TIME 24 HOURS`, "UNKNOWN TIME"),

          # Pad to 4 digits with leading zeros
          # e.g. "140" -&gt; "0140", "10" -&gt; "0010", NA -&gt; "0000"
          `TIME 24 HOURS` = case_when(
            is.na(`TIME 24 HOURS`) ~ "0000",
            TRUE ~ str_pad(`TIME 24 HOURS`, width = 4, 
                           side = "left", pad = "0")
          ),

          # Convert to a proper time type (hhmm format)
          TIME_PARSED = hm(paste0(
            str_sub(`TIME 24 HOURS`, 1, 2), ":",
            str_sub(`TIME 24 HOURS`, 3, 4)
          ))
        )
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Before moving on, let's check the conversion did what we expected.
        Each value of <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> should map to exactly one
        <c>TIME_PARSED</c> value, with no surprises:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
      group_by(`TIME 24 HOURS`, TIME_PARSED) %&gt;%
      count() %&gt;%
      View()
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        If any value of <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> has two or more <c>TIME_PARSED</c>
        entries, then the padding or parsing has done something unexpected, and
        we'd need to look into it.
        If everything looks right, we can move on.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now we can check the result another way, by plotting the number of
        crashes in each hour, and seeing whether the pattern looks sensible:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
        mutate(HOUR = hour(TIME_PARSED))

      ggplot(kenya_accidents_database, aes(x = HOUR)) +
        geom_bar() +
        labs(title = "Crashes by hour of day",
             x = "Hour (24-hour clock)",
             y = "Number of incidents")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        We'd expect to see a peak in the evening, which is what Muguro et al.
        (2020) found in their five-year dataset, and what we saw in the hourly
        chart from their paper in <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>.
        If the pattern looks broadly right, that's a good sign our padding
        assumption was correct.
        If it looked really wrong (say, a spike at 3am and nothing during the
        day), that would tell us to go back and question the assumption.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4_img1_crashes">
        <caption>
          Crashes by hour of day, Kenya Road Accidents database, April <ndash/>
          June 2016.
          Crashes peak between 19:00 and 21:00, consistent with evening rush
          hour.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_img1_crashes.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart showing the number of fatal road accidents by hour of
              the day on the 24-hour clock.
              Counts are generally low between 2-5am, then rise gradually
              through the morning and afternoon.
              Counts rise sharply from hour 18 onwards, peaking at around 46 at
              hour 20, then declining through hours 21 and 22 before rising
              slightly again at hour 23.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        One thing to watch out for: the <c>NA</c>s we set to <c>0000</c> will
        now look like crashes at midnight.
        If you're analysing time-of-day patterns, it's a good idea to either
        leave them out or flag them separately, because they're values we've
        filled in, and we don't actually know those times.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-county">
      <title>Fixing county names</title>

      <p>
        The time column had implausible values: entries we had to interpret
        before we could use them.
        <c>COUNTY</c> has a different kind of problem.
        Here, the values are inconsistent.
        If the same county is spelled two different ways, any analysis you run
        will treat it as two different counties.
        A pivot table on <c>COUNTY</c> makes these really easy to spot, because
        the names are listed alphabetically, so near-duplicates end up next to
        each other:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        rpivotTable(kenya_accidents_database, rows = "COUNTY")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Two problems jump out straight away: <c>KERCHO</c> and <c>KERICHO</c>
        are listed separately, and so are <c>MURANGA</c> and <c>MURANG'A</c>.
        These are obviously the same counties, entered in different ways (see
        the left image in <xref ref="fig-ch4-counties-1"/>).
        We can fix them with <c>case_when()</c>:
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-counties-1">
        <caption>
          Pivot table of <c>COUNTY</c> counts before (left) and after (right)
          cleaning.
          The left panel shows <c>KERCHO</c> and <c>KERICHO</c> as separate
          entries (highlighted in orange), and <c>MURANG'A</c> and
          <c>MURANGA</c> as separate entries (also highlighted).
          The right panel shows these have been merged into single entries after
          cleaning.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_img2_counties_1.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Two side-by-side pivot tables showing county counts.
              The left table, representing the data before cleaning, has orange
              highlighting around two pairs of duplicate entries: KERCHO with 1
              incident and KERICHO with 5 incidents; and MURANG'A with 3
              incidents and MURANGA with 3 incidents.
              The right table, representing the data after cleaning, shows
              KERCHO merged into KERICHO with 6 total incidents, and MURANGA
              merged into MURANG'A with 6 total incidents.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(COUNTY = case_when(
            COUNTY == "KERCHO"  ~ "KERICHO",
            COUNTY == "MURANGA" ~ "MURANG'A",
            TRUE ~ COUNTY
          ))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Now pull up the pivot table again, and the duplicates should be gone
        (see the right image in <xref ref="fig-ch4-counties-1"/>).
        It's really worth doing this check every time, instead of just assuming
        the fix worked.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        rpivotTable(kenya_accidents_database, rows = "COUNTY")
        </code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-base">
      <title>Fixing base/sub-base names</title>

      <p>
        The <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> column has the same problem: different
        spellings of the same place, and some entries starting with <c>DTEO</c>,
        as you can see in <xref ref="fig-ch4_img2_base"/>.
        We think <c>DTEO</c> stands for the District Traffic Enforcement Officer
        responsible for that area, which would make <c>DTEO DAGORETTI</c> the
        same as <c>DAGORETTI</c>.
        But in practice, you'd want to check this with someone who knows the
        data before removing it, just in case the prefix means something that
        matters for your analysis.
        For this example, we'll assume it doesn't:
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4_img2_base">
        <caption>
          Frequency of Base/Sub-base values in the data (shown via pivot table).
          Several entries refer to the same location under different spellings.
          For example, <c>DAGORETTI</c>, <c>DAGORETI</c>, and
          <c>DTEO DAGORETTI</c> all appear as separate entries and need to be
          standardised before analysis.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_img2_base.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A pivot table listing Base/Sub-base values alphabetically with
              their counts.
              Several spelling variants of the same location are visible as
              separate entries, including DAGORETI, DAGORETTI, and DTEO
              DAGORETTI.
              Other variants visible include MOIS BRIDGE and MOI'S BRIDGE, and
              SALGA and SALGAA.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(
            `BASE/SUB BASE` = str_trim(`BASE/SUB BASE`),
            `BASE/SUB BASE` = str_remove(`BASE/SUB BASE`, 
                                          regex("^DTEO\\s+", ignore_case = TRUE)),
            `BASE/SUB BASE` = case_when(
              `BASE/SUB BASE` == "DAGORETTI"   ~ "DAGORETI",
              `BASE/SUB BASE` == "MOI'S BRIDGE" ~ "MOIS BRIDGE",
              `BASE/SUB BASE` == "MAKINGENI"   ~ "MAKONGENI",
              `BASE/SUB BASE` == "MURANGA"     ~ "MURANG'A",
              `BASE/SUB BASE` == "SALGAA"      ~ "SALGA",
              TRUE ~ `BASE/SUB BASE`
            )
          )
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Now we can use the relationship between <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> and
        <c>COUNTY</c> as another quality check.
        A county can have lots of sub-bases, but we'd expect each sub-base to
        belong to just one county.
        So if a sub-base shows up in two different counties, that's a sign
        something might be wrong.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        rpivotTable(kenya_accidents_database,
                    rows = "BASE/SUB BASE",
                    cols = "COUNTY")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-counties-2">
        <caption>
          Cross-tabulation of Base/Sub-base against County (selected columns
          shown).
          This view reveals two problems.
          First, NAKURU base has 12 total incidents: 11 in Nakuru county and 1
          in <c>MAKURU</c>, which is almost certainly a typo.
          Second, NYANDO base has 4 incidents recorded in Homa Bay and 4 in
          Kisumu.
          A quick lookup confirms that Nyando is in Kisumu county, but it is
          worth asking whether the Homa Bay entries are a typo, or whether there
          is a separate place with a similar name in Homa Bay that was also
          recorded as NYANDO.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_img2_counties_2.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A pivot table cross-tabulating Base/Sub-base values in rows
              against County in columns, with counts in each cell.
              Only selected county columns are shown.
              Key observations visible in the table: NAKURU base has 11
              incidents in Nakuru county and 1 in Makuru county, totalling 12;
              Embakasi base has 11 incidents all in Nairobi county; Nyando base
              has 4 incidents in Homa Bay and 4 in Kisumu, totalling 8; Makindu
              base has 8 incidents all in Makueni county; Starehe base has 8
              incidents all in Nairobi county.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        If we sort by count, we can see that <c>NAKURU</c> has 12 cases: 11 in
        Nakuru county, and 1 in <c>MAKURU</c>.
        That's almost certainly a typo, so we fix it the same way as before.
        We love this example, because it shows how cleaning one variable can
        reveal a problem in another.
        We weren't even looking at <c>COUNTY</c> at this point, we were looking
        at <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c>, but the cross-tabulation brought it to the
        surface.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(COUNTY = case_when(
            COUNTY == "MAKURU" ~ "NAKURU",
            TRUE ~ COUNTY
          ))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        We can also see that <c>NYANDO</c> appears in both Homa Bay and Kisumu
        counties.
        A quick check tells us that Nyando is in Kisumu county, so the Homa Bay
        entries are probably errors.
        But it's worth going back to the original source before correcting them.
        Could it be that <c>NYANDO</c> is the mistake, and Homa Bay is right?
        The data alone can't tell us.
        This is exactly the kind of question where you need someone who knows
        the area, or access to the original records, and no amount of statistics
        will fix it for you.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-dates">
      <title>Dates: converting and checking</title>

      <p>
        Next up is the <c>Date DD/MM/YYYY</c> column.
        Before we can do anything useful with it in R, we need to convert it
        from text into a proper date type.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(`Date DD/MM/YYYY` = as.Date(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Once it's converted, a simple bar chart shows us something we weren't
        expecting:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(kenya_accidents_database,
               aes(x = `Date DD/MM/YYYY`)) +
          geom_bar()
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-dates">
        <caption>
          Number of fatal road accidents per day, Kenya Road Accidents database,
          April <ndash/> June 2016.
          Note the complete absence of data in May, suggesting this month was
          not included in the extract rather than that no crashes occurred.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_dates.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart showing the count of fatal road accidents for each
              date in the dataset.
              Data is present for April and June but entirely absent for May,
              leaving a clear gap in the middle of the plot.
              Within April, there is a notable spike of 19 incidents on one day
              in mid-April, with most other days recording between 3 and 12
              incidents.
              The data in April appears to end around late April with two bars
              close together near the end of the month before the May gap.
              June data begins in early June and runs to approximately the 26th,
              with counts generally between 3 and 12 per day and a spike of
              around 12 in mid-June.
              Both April and June show gaps of zero incidents on certain dates,
              most notably towards the end of each month.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        There are no crashes in May at all!
        The data jumps straight from April to June.
        It's very unlikely that there were no crashes in May.
        Much more likely, the May data just wasn't included in this extract.
        We can't fix that, but it's really important to know about: any analysis
        of monthly patterns from this dataset would be misleading, because it
        isn't a complete three-month record.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can also check the date column by looking at patterns across the days
        of the week.
        To do this, we pull out the weekday from each date:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(weekday = lubridate::wday(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`, label = TRUE))

        ggplot(kenya_accidents_database,
               aes(x = weekday)) +
          geom_bar()
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-weekday">
        <caption>
          Number of fatal road accidents by day of week, Kenya Road Accidents
          database, April <ndash/> June 2016.
          Saturday has by far the highest count, followed by Friday, while
          Monday has the fewest.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_weekday.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart showing the total count of fatal road accidents for
              each day of the week.
              Saturday has the highest count at just over 100, followed by
              Friday at around 70.
              Sunday and Wednesday are roughly equal at around 48 each.
              Tuesday and Thursday are similar at around 42 and 38 respectively.
              Monday has the lowest count at approximately 28.
              The pattern shows a clear increase towards the end of the week,
              peaking on Saturday.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Fridays and Saturdays have the most crashes, and Mondays have the
        fewest.
        That fits with what Kenya's National Road Safety Action Plan reports:
        nearly half of all fatal crashes happen between Friday and Sunday.
        But reporting delays could also play a part here.
        A crash late on one day might be logged the next day, which could shift
        some of the Friday peak into Saturday.
        We also notice gaps on the 27th and 28th of both April and June: two
        days in a row with no crashes recorded, in both months.
        That seems unlikely, and is worth checking against the original data
        source.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-individual">
      <title>Moving to the individual level (Advanced)</title>

      <p>
        Some variables in this dataset describe the incident, like the road, the
        time, the county, and the cause.
        Others describe the individual victim, like their age, gender, and
        category.
        But the data we downloaded is at the incident level, and sometimes
        several victims are squashed into a single row (see <xref
        ref="table-kenya-accidents-snippet"/> for a snippet of the data).
        As we saw in <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>, mixing levels like this
        makes analysis harder, and can lead to mistakes.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-kenya-accidents-snippet">
        <title>
          A snippet of rows from the Kenya Road Accidents database after initial
          cleaning (Note that <c>NO.</c> is the number of individuals involved in the
          crash)
        </title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <col halign="right"/>
          <col halign="right"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="right"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="right"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell/>
            <cell>TIME 24 HOURS</cell>
            <cell>BASE/SUB BASE</cell>
            <cell>COUNTY</cell>
            <cell>ROAD</cell>
            <cell>PLACE</cell>
            <cell>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</cell>
            <cell>GENDER</cell>
            <cell>AGE</cell>
            <cell>CAUSE CODE</cell>
            <cell>VICTIM</cell>
            <cell>NO.</cell>
            <cell>Date DD/MM/YYYY</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>66</cell>
            <cell>2015</cell>
            <cell>Bureti</cell>
            <cell>Kericho</cell>
            <cell>Buret Ngoina</cell>
            <cell>Ngoina Junction</cell>
            <cell>
              The Probox was overtaking unknown M/V hence collided head on with
              the petrol tanker
            </cell>
            <cell>M</cell>
            <cell>A</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
            <cell>7 Passengers 1 Driver</cell>
            <cell>8</cell>
            <cell>2016-06-16</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>224</cell>
            <cell>2020</cell>
            <cell>Vihiga</cell>
            <cell>Vihiga</cell>
            <cell>Chavakali Kapsabet</cell>
            <cell>Musasa Area</cell>
            <cell>Head on collision</cell>
            <cell>M</cell>
            <cell>A</cell>
            <cell>8</cell>
            <cell>Passenger</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>2016-04-21</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>327</cell>
            <cell>2205</cell>
            <cell>Nanyuki</cell>
            <cell>NA</cell>
            <cell>Kinamba</cell>
            <cell>Ngarua</cell>
            <cell>The vehicle overturned killing 6 victims</cell>
            <cell>4F &amp; INF</cell>
            <cell>A</cell>
            <cell>26</cell>
            <cell>Passenger</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>2016-04-08</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>86</cell>
            <cell>1920</cell>
            <cell>Kangundo</cell>
            <cell>Makueni</cell>
            <cell>Kangundo Nairobi</cell>
            <cell>Kalaah Kware Bridge</cell>
            <cell>The vehicle lost control and veered off the road</cell>
            <cell>2M &amp; 2F</cell>
            <cell>A</cell>
            <cell>26</cell>
            <cell>4 Passengers 1 Driver</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>2016-06-12</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>117</cell>
            <cell>1800</cell>
            <cell>Makueni</cell>
            <cell>Makueni</cell>
            <cell>Wote Makindu</cell>
            <cell>Kisene Bridge</cell>
            <cell>
              The vehicle lost control and plunged into the Miseke Bridge
            </cell>
            <cell>M</cell>
            <cell>A</cell>
            <cell>26</cell>
            <cell>Passenger</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>2016-06-08</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        A cleaner approach is to create two linked data frames: one at the
        incident level, and one at the individual level.
        Why?
        It comes down to what each variable actually describes.
        Some variables belong to the crash itself (the road, the time, the
        county, the cause code, the date), and these are the same for everyone
        involved in that crash.
        Other variables belong to each victim (their age, gender, and category),
        and these can be different for each person in the same crash.
      </p>

      <p>
        Keeping everything in one data frame causes problems in both directions.
        If we convert <c>AGE</c> to a numeric variable, R will turn entries like
        <c>"A &amp; J"</c> in <c>AGE</c> into <c>NA</c>, but those aren't
        missing values, they're several people.
        But if we expand each row so that every person gets their own row, then
        crash-level variables like <c>ROAD</c> and <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> get
        copied for every victim of the same crash, and counting crashes
        correctly becomes much harder.
        The solution is to separate the two levels, link them with a shared ID,
        and work with each one at the right level, just like we discussed in
        <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>.
        We start by adding an incident ID, so that once the levels are
        separated, we can link them back together:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(incident_id = 1:nrow(kenya_accidents_database))

        kenya_individual_level &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          dplyr::select(incident_id, AGE, GENDER, `NO.`, VICTIM)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The <c>GENDER</c> column has entries like <c>"2M &amp; 2F"</c> or <c>"4F
        &amp; INF"</c>, where several people are packed into one cell.
        To get one row per person, we need to expand these.
        This kind of string-splitting is fiddly, so it's perfectly reasonable to
        write a helper function (or ask an AI tool to draft one, and then check
        it really carefully):
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # Helper to split entries separated by commas, "&amp;", or "and"
        split_flexible &lt;- function(x) {
          str_split(x, "\\s*(?:[,&amp;]|and)\\s*")
        }

        kenya_individual_level &lt;- kenya_individual_level %&gt;%
          mutate(
            AGE    = split_flexible(AGE),
            GENDER = split_flexible(GENDER),
            VICTIM = split_flexible(VICTIM)
          ) %&gt;%
          mutate(
            AGE    = map2(AGE,    `NO.`, rep_len),
            GENDER = map2(GENDER, `NO.`, rep_len),
            VICTIM = map2(VICTIM, `NO.`, rep_len)
          ) %&gt;%
          unnest(cols = c(AGE, GENDER, VICTIM))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        There's a limit to what this can do, though.
        When genders and ages are mixed in the same incident, the expansion
        can't tell us which gender goes with which age.
        We know an incident involved, say, two men and two women, but we don't
        know which of the four victims was which.
        That's a limitation of the original data, and no amount of cleaning can
        get that information back.
        For incidents where <c>NO.</c> (the number of victims) is more than one
        and the victim types are mixed, the safest thing to do is handle them by
        hand.
        There are only a handful of these in the dataset, so that's doable.
        The key thing throughout is to make sure you don't accidentally link an
        age or gender to the wrong type of victim.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <c>AGE</c> column is a real mix: numeric ages, the letter <c>A</c>
        (adult), the letter <c>J</c> (junior), and entries like <c>"5
        MONTHS"</c>.
        We clean these in two steps.
        First, we convert ages in months to decimal years.
        Then we create a categorical <c>AGE_GROUP</c> variable that keeps the
        <c>A</c> and <c>J</c> information:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # Convert "5 MONTHS" to 0.42, etc.
        clean_months &lt;- function(df, column_name, format = "decimal") {
          col &lt;- sym(column_name)
          pattern &lt;- "(?i)(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?)\\s*(?:months?|mth|mths)"
          df %&gt;%
            mutate(
              .months = as.numeric(str_match(!!col, pattern)[, 2]),
              !!col := case_when(
                !is.na(.months) &amp; format == "decimal" ~
                  as.character(round(.months / 12, 2)),
                TRUE ~ as.character(!!col)
              )
            ) %&gt;%
            select(-.months)
        }

        kenya_individual_level &lt;- clean_months(kenya_individual_level, 
                                               "AGE", format = "decimal")

        # Create AGE_GROUP from A/J codes and numeric ages
        kenya_individual_level &lt;- kenya_individual_level %&gt;%
          mutate(
            .age_num = suppressWarnings(as.numeric(AGE)),
            AGE_GROUP = case_when(
              AGE %in% c("A", "ADULT")    ~ "ADULT",
              AGE %in% c("J", "JUNIOR")   ~ "JUNIOR",
              !is.na(.age_num) &amp; .age_num &gt;= 18 ~ "ADULT",
              !is.na(.age_num) &amp; .age_num &lt;  18 ~ "JUNIOR",
              TRUE ~ NA_character_
            )
          ) %&gt;%
          select(-.age_num)

        # Now convert AGE to numeric (A, J become NA - that is expected)
        kenya_individual_level$AGE &lt;- as.numeric(kenya_individual_level$AGE)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        We can check that <c>AGE_GROUP</c> worked by cross-tabulating it against
        <c>AGE</c> in a pivot table:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    rpivotTable(kenya_individual_level, rows = "AGE_GROUP", cols = "AGE")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The result is in <xref ref="fig-ch4-agegroup-by-age"/>, and the grouping
        has worked as expected.
        All the numeric ages under 18 are in the <c>JUNIOR</c> row, and all the
        ages 18 and over are in the <c>ADULT</c> row.
        And the <c>null</c> row (where no group was assigned) is empty, so every
        row has been given a group.
        The rows where <c>AGE</c> was only recorded as <c>A</c> or <c>J</c> show
        up in the <c>null</c> <em>column</em> instead, because they couldn't be
        turned into a number, but they still have the right <c>AGE_GROUP</c>,
        which is exactly what we'd expect.
        Nothing has been lost, and nothing has been put in the wrong group.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-agegroup-by-age">
        <caption>
          Cross-tabulation of <c>AGE_GROUP</c> against numeric <c>AGE</c>,
          confirming that the grouping was applied correctly.
          All ages below 18 fall in the <c>JUNIOR</c> row and all ages 18 and
          above fall in the <c>ADULT</c> row.
          The <c>null</c> row is empty, indicating no cases were left ungrouped
          among those with a numeric age.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_agegroup_by_age.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A pivot table with AGE_GROUP in rows and numeric AGE values as
              column headers.
              The ADULT row contains counts only for ages 18 and above, with
              values such as 3 at age 18, 5 at age 19, and increasing counts at
              ages 25, 30, and 35.
              The JUNIOR row contains counts only for ages below 18, including
              ages 0.25, 0.42, 0.75, 1.5, and whole numbers from 3 to 17.
              The null row is empty throughout.
              The Totals row matches the sum of ADULT and JUNIOR for each age,
              confirming no observations were missed or miscategorised.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        We can also check the numeric ages with a bar chart:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    ggplot(kenya_individual_level, aes(x = AGE)) +
      geom_bar()
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The plot in <xref ref="fig-ch4-age-bar"/> shows a really striking
        pattern.
        There are big spikes at ages 30, 35, and 40, and smaller spikes at 50,
        60, and 70 compared with the ages around them.
        At first glance, you might think road crash victims in Kenya are
        unusually likely to be exactly 30 or exactly 35!
        The much more likely explanation is that the people recording the data
        were rounding ages to the nearest 5 or 10.
        This happens a lot with self-reported or administrative age data.
        It's a great example of data that isn't exactly wrong, but also isn't as
        precise as it looks.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-age-bar">
        <caption>
          Distribution of victim ages in the Kenya Road Accidents database,
          April <ndash/> June 2016.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_age_bar.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart showing the count of road accident victims by age.
              Pronounced spikes are visible at ages 30, 35, 40, 50, 60, and 70,
              consistent with age rounding in the original data.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This is one reason why <c>AGE_GROUP</c>, which only separates adults
        from juniors, might actually be more honest for this dataset than
        treating <c>AGE</c> as a precise number.
        Having said that, putting everyone over 18 into one <q>adult</q> group
        loses real information.
        A 19-year-old and a 70-year-old are very different road users!
        A sensible middle ground might be broader age bands, like 18 <ndash/>
        30, 31 <ndash/> 40, and so on, which keep meaningful differences without
        pretending to a precision that was never there.
        The right choice depends on the question you're trying to answer.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can also look at the ages with a quick summary and a boxplot:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        summary(kenya_individual_level$AGE)
        boxplot(kenya_individual_level$AGE)
        
        #&gt;    Min. 1st Qu.  Median    Mean 3rd Qu.    Max.    NA's 
        #&gt;    0.25   22.00   30.00   31.41   40.00   90.00     229
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Ages range from 0.25 (3 months) to 90 years, with a median of 30.
        The minimum and maximum both look plausible.
        And the 229 missing values are what we'd expect: they're the rows where
        age was only recorded as <c>A</c> or <c>J</c>, which we can't turn into
        a number without making assumptions we have no right to make.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-accident-category">
      <title>Recoding accident descriptions</title>

      <p>
        The <c>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</c> column has 148 different free-text
        descriptions.
        Lots of them describe the same kind of event (a head-on collision, a hit
        and run, losing control), just written differently each time.
        To make this column useful, we need to recode it into a smaller set of
        categories.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the kind of task where generative AI tools can be really
        helpful.
        We don't want AI making the decisions for us, but it's great for
        drafting a first set of categories, which we can then check and correct
        ourselves.
        That's how the code below was written: AI suggested the first groupings,
        and then we went through them by hand against the actual entries.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(ACCIDENT_CATEGORY = case_when(

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("HEAD ON|HEAD-ON", ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Head on collision",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("HIT.*R(A|U)N|HIT &amp; R(A|U)N", ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Hit and run",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("LOST CONTROL|LOSE CONTROL|TYRE BURST|STEERING ROD|POWER FAIL",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Lost control",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("KNOCKED DOWN|KNOCK DOWN|RAN OVER|RUN OVER|KNOCKED PEDESTRIAN",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Knocked down",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("REAR|FROM BEHIND|FAILED TO KEEP DISTANCE",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Rear collision",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("HIT.*M/CYCLE|HIT.*MOTOR CYCLE|HIT.*CYCLIST|HIT.*CYCLE",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Vehicle hit motorcycle/cyclist",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("OVERTOOK|OVERTAKING|OVERTAK",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Overtaking incident",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("ROLL|OVERTURN", ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Rolled/overturned",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("VEERED OFF|VEER OFF|LANDED INTO A DITCH|LANDED IN A DITCH|PLUNGED|PLUGGED INTO",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Veered off road",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("ALIGHT|ALIGHTED|JUMPED OFF|FELL DOWN AND WAS",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Alighting/boarding incident",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("RAMMED INTO|RAMMED", ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Vehicle rammed into another",

            TRUE ~ "Other/unknown"
          ))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        When we reviewed the result in a pivot table, with both
        <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> and <c>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</c> in the rows,
        we found a handful of entries in the wrong category.
        For example, <c>"THE VEHICLE OVERTOOK A M/CYCLE AND LOST CONTROL VEERING
        OFF THE ROAD &amp; ROLLED"</c> had been put in Lost control, but it's
        really better described as an overtaking incident.
        And <c>"THE MOTOR CYCLE HIT THE UNKNOWN M/V LOOSING CONTROL"</c> (spot
        the typo!) wasn't picked up by the lost control pattern at all.
        We fix these in a second pass.
        Putting <c>TRUE ~ ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> at the end keeps everything we
        assigned before, and only changes the specific cases we want to change:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(ACCIDENT_CATEGORY = case_when(

            # Overtaking takes priority over lost control
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("OVERTOOK|OVERTAKING|OVERTAK", 
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Overtaking incident",

            # Veered off road + knocked down stays in veered off road
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("VEERED OFF|VEER OFF", ignore_case = TRUE)) &amp;
              str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                         regex("KNOCKED DOWN|KNOCK DOWN", 
                               ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Veered off road",

            # Catches "LOOSING CONTROL" typo
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("LOST CONTROL|LOSE CONTROL|LOOSING CONTROL|LOSING CONTROL|TYRE BURST|STEERING ROD|POWER FAIL",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Lost control",

            # Collided with goes to head on collision
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("COLLIDED WITH", ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Head on collision",

            # Knocked down, excluding cycle knocking down a vehicle
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("KNOCKED DOWN|KNOCK DOWN|RAN OVER|RUN OVER",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) &amp;
              !str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                          regex("CYCLE KNOCKED DOWN THE VEHICLE", 
                                ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Knocked down",

            # Rammed, including cycle knocking down a vehicle
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("RAMMED|CYCLE KNOCKED DOWN THE VEHICLE",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Vehicle rammed into another",

            # Fell and was run over goes to other
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("FELL DOWN AND WAS OVER RAN", 
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Other/unknown",

            # Hit a pedestrian goes to knocked down
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("HIT.*PEDESTRIAN|HIT A PEDESTRIAN", 
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Knocked down",

            # Keep everything else as previously assigned
            TRUE ~ ACCIDENT_CATEGORY
          ))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The order of the conditions really matters here, just like in the first
        pass.
        <c>case_when()</c> uses the first rule that matches, and then stops.
        So by putting overtaking before lost control, a crash description that
        mentions both gets classed as overtaking, which is the more specific and
        more informative category.
      </p>

      <p>
        And then we check the result by pulling up the pivot table again, with
        both <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> and <c>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</c> in the
        rows:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    rpivotTable(kenya_accidents_database, 
                rows = c("ACCIDENT_CATEGORY", "BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS"))
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-accidents-check">
        <caption>
          Pivot table showing accident categories alongside the original brief
          accident descriptions, allowing manual verification of the
          categorisation.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_accidents_check.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A pivot table showing accident categories in the left column
              alongside the original brief accident descriptions in the middle
              column, with total counts on the right.
              Categories shown include: Alighting/boarding incident (4 entries,
              e.g.
              "The victim alighted while the vehicle was in motion"); Head on
              collision (63 entries across 10 descriptions, dominated by "HEAD
              ON COLLISION" with 51 incidents); Hit and run (54 entries across 4
              descriptions, dominated by "HIT AND RUN" with 46 incidents); and
              Knocked down (partial, showing 16 descriptions including
              pedestrians, cyclists, and motorcyclists being knocked down by
              various vehicles).
              The table illustrates how the free-text descriptions have been
              grouped into consistent categories, while also revealing remaining
              spelling variants within categories such as "HIT AND RAN" and "HIT
              &amp; RAN" alongside "HIT AND RUN".
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        When you look through the result, you might find entries you would have
        categorised differently.
        And someone else might disagree with <em>your</em> choices!
        Turning free text into categories is always a judgement call.
        There's rarely one objectively correct answer, only choices that are
        more or less sensible, and more or less useful for the analysis you're
        doing.
        What really matters is that you make those choices on purpose, and
        openly.
        And notice that we haven't overwritten the original <c>BRIEF ACCIDENT
        DETAILS</c> column, so if you or a collaborator disagrees with a
        category, the original text is still there to go back to.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-payoff">
      <title>What the cleaning unlocks</title>

      <p>
        Now we have <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> and <c>weekday</c>, two variables
        we created while cleaning, we can start asking questions we couldn't
        have asked with the raw data.
        For example, are some types of accident more likely on certain days of
        the week?
        A question like this does two jobs at once.
        It's interesting in its own right, and it's also a check on the dates.
        If the weekday patterns looked completely random, with no difference at
        all between a Monday and a Saturday, that might be a sign something went
        wrong with how the dates were recorded or converted.
        If the pattern looks plausible, that gives us some confidence that the
        dates are broadly right.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(kenya_accidents_database,
               aes(x = weekday, fill = ACCIDENT_CATEGORY)) +
          geom_bar(position = "fill") +
          labs(y = "Proportion", x = "Day of week",
               fill = "Accident category")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-bar-accident-weekday">
        <caption>
          Proportion of each accident category by day of week, Kenya Road
          Accidents database, April <ndash/> June 2016.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_bar_accident_weekday.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A stacked proportional bar chart with day of week on the x-axis
              and proportion on the y-axis.
              Each bar is divided into twelve accident categories shown in
              different colours.
              Hit and run (olive green) and knocked down (bright green) dominate
              most days.
              Vehicle rammed into another (pink) appears as a notably larger
              proportion on Saturdays compared to weekdays.
              Head on collision (orange) is consistently present across all
              days.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This figure shows the proportion of each accident category on each day
        of the week.
        Hit and run and knocked down are the biggest categories every day, but
        there are some differences worth noticing.
        Vehicle rammed into another makes up a bigger share of crashes on
        Saturdays than on weekdays.
        That might fit with drink driving, but the dataset is too small for us
        to say anything firm.
        We can also flip it round, with accident category on the x-axis and day
        of the week as the fill:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    ggplot(kenya_accidents_database,
           aes(x = ACCIDENT_CATEGORY, fill = weekday)) +
      geom_bar(position = "fill") +
      coord_flip() +
      labs(y = "Proportion", x = "Accident category",
           fill = "Day of week")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-bar-accident-weekday-2">
        <caption>
          Proportion of each day of week within each accident category, Kenya
          Road Accidents database, April <ndash/> June 2016.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_bar_accident_weekday_2.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A stacked proportional bar chart with accident category on the
              x-axis and proportion on the y-axis.
              Each bar is divided into seven days of the week shown in a colour
              scale from dark purple (Sunday) to yellow (Saturday).
              Saturday (yellow) makes up a notably large proportion of the
              Vehicle rammed into another and Lost control categories.
              Overtaking incidents appear relatively more common on Thursdays
              and Fridays.
              Most categories show a broadly even spread across weekdays.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This view makes it easier to see which categories happen more at
        weekends or on weekdays.
        Overtaking incidents, for example, seem to be more concentrated on
        Thursdays and Fridays, which might be because there's more traffic at
        the end of the working week.
        Again, at this stage, these are exploratory observations, and we
        shouldn't treat them as firm conclusions.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-conclusion">
      <title>Summary</title>

      <p>
        Notice that we created both <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> and <c>weekday</c>
        while cleaning.
        The exploration we just did was only possible because of that cleaning.
        And it works the other way round too, because exploring is what led us
        to clean.
        We only noticed May was missing because we plotted the dates.
        We only saw how many variants needed combining because we looked at the
        accident descriptions in a pivot table.
        So think of cleaning and exploring as a cycle, and expect to go back and
        forth between them all the way through an analysis.
      </p>

      <p>
        And you can keep going with this data!
        For example, looking at <c>NO.</c> (the number of victims in each
        incident) against <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> gives some useful flags.
        An alighting or boarding incident with only one victim makes sense.
        A hit and run with two victims is plausible.
        Checks like these won't always find errors, but they point you to cases
        that are worth a second look, and that's exactly what validation is for.
      </p>

      <p>
        To finish, let's summarise the different kinds of cleaning we've done in
        this chapter, because they're different problems, and you'll come across
        all of them again and again in real data:
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-cleaning-approaches">
        <title>Types of data cleaning and examples from this chapter</title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Type of problem</cell>
            <cell>What to look for</cell>
            <cell>Example from this chapter</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Implausible values</cell>
            <cell>
              Values that cannot be correct given what the variable represents
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> entries with 1, 2, or 3 digits instead of 4;
              leading zeros stripped by Excel
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Inconsistent values</cell>
            <cell>The same thing recorded in different ways</cell>
            <cell>
              <c>KERCHO</c> vs <c>KERICHO</c>; <c>HIT AND RAN</c> vs
              <c>HIT AND RUN</c>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Missing values</cell>
            <cell>Values that are absent or encoded as text placeholders</cell>
            <cell>
              <c>UNKNOWN TIME</c> in <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c>; <c>NA</c> s in county
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Wrong level of analysis</cell>
            <cell>Multiple observations encoded in a single row</cell>
            <cell>
              <c>"2M &amp; 2F"</c> in <c>GENDER</c>;
              <c>"7 PASSENGERS 1 DRIVER"</c> in <c>VICTIM</c>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Recoding free text</cell>
            <cell>
              Unstructured descriptions that need grouping before analysis
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <c>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</c> collapsed into
              <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Wrong data type</cell>
            <cell>
              Variables stored as the wrong type for what they represent
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Dates stored as text; ages stored as characters including codes
              like <c>A</c> and <c>J</c>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Silent transformation</cell>
            <cell>Values changed by software without warning</cell>
            <cell>
              Leading zeros stripped from time values by Excel; gene names
              converted to dates
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        One last thing.
        Several decisions in this chapter meant going beyond the data itself to
        check a choice.
        We looked up whether Nyando is in Kisumu or Homa Bay county.
        We assumed the leading-zero explanation for the times, and said that in
        a real project, we'd check it with whoever looks after the data.
        This kind of outside checking (against a map, a published source, a
        local expert, or the people who collected the data) is a normal and
        necessary part of data cleaning.
        Sometimes, the only way to find out whether a value is wrong is to look
        beyond the data.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-cleaning-recoding">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <!-- Conceptual: Ebola -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-ebola-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="ss-ebola-collection"/>, patient data had to be read out
          across a fence, photographed, or copied from damaged forms, and many of
          the errors were only found in an audit months later.
          Suggest one practical check, applied at the point of data entry rather
          than afterwards, that could have caught transcription errors while the
          data was still being used to make decisions during the outbreak.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- Conceptual: PHE -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-phe-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The PHE failure in <xref ref="ss-phe-pipeline"/> was so dangerous
          because it was silent: the file that lost data looked exactly like a
          file that hadn't.
          Suggest one practical check that, if it ran automatically every time a
          file was produced, would have caught this problem within a day, rather
          than a week.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- Conceptual: gene names -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-gene-names">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The gene name problem in <xref ref="sec-gene-names"/> had been
          documented, with workarounds, as early as 2004, and yet it carried on
          unnoticed across thousands of papers for over a decade.
          Suggest two things that could have been done differently to catch or
          prevent it earlier: one for individual researchers, and one for
          journal editors or peer reviewers.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: missing values -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-mics-linking">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Remember in <xref ref="ss-mics-linking"/>, we found 183 women whose
          household ID didn't match any household in the household file?
          That shouldn't be possible: every woman in the women's data should
          have a matching household in the household file.
          Have a go at investigating what might have gone wrong.
          For example, could there be a typo in the household ID?
          Does the data get cut off after a certain number of rows?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: inconsistent values -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-county-names">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The next few exercises use the Kenya Road Accidents database from this
          chapter.
          Download it from <url
          href="https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database">data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database</url>
          and load it into R before you start.
        </p>

        <p>
          Load the Kenya Road Accidents database and inspect the <c>COUNTY</c>
          column.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use <c>sort(unique(kenya_accidents_database$COUNTY))</c> to list
              all unique county values.
              How many distinct values are there?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Identify at least two pairs of values that appear to refer to the
              same county but are recorded inconsistently.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Write R code using <c>case_when()</c> to standardise those values,
              and verify the result by running <c>sort(unique(...))</c> again.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: implausible values -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-time-values">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Inspect the <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> column in the Kenya Road Accidents
          database.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              How many distinct values does the column contain?
              Use <c>group_by()</c> and <c>count()</c> to list them with their
              frequencies.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Identify at least three values that appear implausible or
              ambiguous, and explain what you think each one might actually
              represent.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Apply the leading-zero padding fix described in this chapter and
              convert the result to an hour variable using <c>lubridate</c>.
              Plot the distribution of crashes by hour and describe what you
              see.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: missing values -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-missing-values">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The Kenya Road Accidents database contains missing values in several
          columns.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Write R code to count the number of missing values in each column.
              Which column has the most missing values?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The <c>AGE</c> column has both true missing values and coded
              values like <c>A</c> and <c>J</c>.
              After converting <c>AGE</c> to numeric, how many <c>NA</c> s
              result?
              Are all of these genuinely unknown ages, or are some of them
              something else?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Explain in your own words the difference between a value that is
              missing and a value that is unknown.
              Does that distinction matter for this dataset?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: recoding -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-victim-recode">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <c>VICTIM</c> column in the Kenya Road Accidents database has some
          inconsistent entries.
          For example, <c>M/CYCLIST</c>, <c>MOTOR CYCLIST</c>, and
          <c>MOTORCYCLIST</c> all show up as separate categories.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use <c>sort(unique(kenya_accidents_database$VICTIM))</c> to list
              all distinct victim categories.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Write R code using <c>case_when()</c> and <c>str_detect()</c> to
              recode the <c>VICTIM</c> column into a smaller set of clean
              categories.
              State clearly what categories you chose and why.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              After recoding, use <c>count()</c> to summarise the distribution
              of victim categories.
              Does the pattern match what Muguro et al.
              found in the five-year dataset?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: levels of analysis -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-individual-level">
      <statement>
        <p>
          This chapter created a separate individual-level data frame from the
          incident-level Kenya Road Accidents database.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              In your own words, explain why keeping age and gender in the
              incident-level data frame creates problems for analysis.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              After expanding the data to the individual level using the code
              from this chapter, how many rows does
              <c>kenya_individual_level</c> have?
              Why is this different from the number of rows in
              <c>kenya_accidents_database</c>?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Using the individual-level data frame, produce a bar chart of
              <c>GENDER</c> and describe what you see.
              Are there any categories that suggest further cleaning is needed?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- Conceptual: cleaning and exploration cycle -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-cycle">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In this chapter, we've argued that data cleaning and data exploration
          work as a cycle, with each one feeding into the other.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Give one example from this chapter where exploration revealed
              something that needed cleaning.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Give one example where cleaning made a new kind of exploration
              possible that was not possible before.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              In the Kenya Road Accidents data, the <c>CAUSE CODE</c> column
              contains numeric codes whose meaning is only partially documented.
              Describe one way you might use exploration to try to work out what
              the undocumented codes mean, without access to the original
              codebook.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>